error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets both central conclusions right. It says APs are generally not paid by the sponsor, and that creation and redemption fees are charged to APs and go to the fund rather than to the AP. It also says creating and redeeming is voluntary and that participant agreements do not require ongoing activity. Its mention of sponsor liquidity or incentive programs as a separate arrangement fits the note that market-maker/LMM contracts are outside AP-status compensation. It has no material error, and the truncated ending loses only a minor point. However, it leaves out the vouched point that sponsors or advisers often waive or absorb creation/redemption fees, especially on custom redemptions. That subsidises the AP's cost and is the key nuance on whether sponsors effectively pay anything. It also omits that agency APs can charge clients for processing creations and redemptions, and describes AP earnings only in dealer terms (arbitrage, spreads, commissions). Leaving out the fee-waiver point is a substantive omission on the payment question, so the grade is partial.\"}","ttft_ms":5161,"type":"result","duration_ms":8138,"uuid":"3719c55e-e0bb-48a9-bf18-910d9699bb3d","ttft_stream_ms":561,"time_to_request_ms":65,"first_content_frame_ms":561,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
